//? Title: BiGAN: Joint Pairs Without a Reconstruction Loss
//? Subtitle: Ordered Gaussian rings, three explicit Adam states and untrained cycle diagnostics
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A small bidirectional GAN fits 400 points from an ordered eight-component Gaussian ring.
A generator maps a two-dimensional Gaussian prior into data coordinates, an encoder maps
data back to code, and a joint discriminator scores(data,E(data)) against(G(prior),prior).
Three independent Adam states perform D, then E, then G writes with a fresh forward after
each update: 39 writes per store and 117 updates, ending at the fixed third epoch.
Reconstruction and latent-cycle paths are diagnostics, with no residual term in fitting.
The short run fails to learn a useful inverse: held-out reconstruction MSE 2.1331 exceeds
the fitting-mean predictor's 2.0098; latent-cycle MSE is 1.2355. Native components, complete
editable orchestration, exact checkpoints, joint-pair controls and independent numerical
checks preserve that weak result rather than claiming convergence or learned invertibility.
)
\toc

> The discriminator sees a pair, not only a point

G receives a prior code z and produces data coordinates. E receives a data point x and
produces code coordinates. D distinguishes the ordered pairs (x,E(x)) and (G(z),z).
Both halves enter D, data first, then code. A real/fake source label appears only in the
loss; there is no supervised class label or reconstruction target in D's forward.
The two occurrences of each network share parameters.

This compact deterministic MLP follows the repository's teaching program. It uses the
joint-pair idea of [Donahue, Krähenbühl and Darrell](https://arxiv.org/abs/1605.09782)
and [Dumoulin et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/1606.00704), with the source's alternating
D–E–G writes. Donahue et al.'s practical protocol also discusses simultaneous updates;
this experiment retains its own explicit schedule and small architecture.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/bigan_method.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
