> Equal marginals need not mean equal joint pairs

Pairs (-1,-1),(1,1) and(-1,1),(1,-1) have identical data and code marginals: each
coordinate takes -1 and 1 equally often. Their pairings differ completely. A discriminator
that sees only one coordinate cannot distinguish these two empirical sources. A joint
nonlinear discriminator can use their relation. This small geometric example explains
the input concatenation; it is not a claim that the random initialized D has learned it.

