> Reversing the source targets sends E and G different gradients

Write a=D(x,E(x)) and b=D(G(z),z), as raw logits with positive meaning data/encoded.
The source objectives, each using a separate pool mean, are

\[
 L_D=\operatorname{mean}sp(-a)+\operatorname{mean}sp(b),\qquad
 L_{EG}=\operatorname{mean}sp(a)+\operatorname{mean}sp(-b).
\]

Here sp is stable softplus. At a=b=0 both losses equal 2log2. The reversed-source
objective is the minimized negative of the inverse objective discussed in
[BiGAN Appendix B](https://arxiv.org/html/1605.09782v7) and
[ALI section 2.3](https://arxiv.org/html/1606.00704v3).
For one pair, its real-branch derivative is σ(a), while its fake-branch derivative is
σ(b)-1. At a=80, b=-80 these stay approximately +1 and -1, so E and G retain strong
source gradients when D confidently distinguishes the two sources.

With D fixed, the real branch depends on E and the fake branch on G. Updating E
therefore leaves the partial derivative of L_EG with respect to G unchanged. D's write
can change both partials. The source explicitly recomputes all three phases anyway.
Neither G(E(x))-x nor E(G(z))-z appears in these fitting objectives.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/bigan_source_gradients.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace n = dm::nn;
  std::vector<double> x, e, g;
  for (int k = -80; k <= 80; ++k) {
    x.push_back(k / 10.);
    auto a           = n::full({1, 1}, k / 10., n::DType::Float64).requires_grad_(),
         b           = n::full({1, 1}, k / 10., n::DType::Float64).requires_grad_();
    auto source      = n::models::LogisticGanObjectives(a, b);
    auto eg          = n::mean(n::softplus(a)) + source.generator;
    auto derivatives = n::grad(eg, {a, b});
    e.push_back(derivatives[0].item());
    g.push_back(derivatives[1].item());
  }
  auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  p.lines(x, e, "Real branch: E direction", {37, 99, 235}, 2.)
      .lines(x, g, "Fake branch: G direction", {220, 38, 38}, 2.)
      .x_label("Respective joint source logit")
      .y_label("Derivative per example")
      .title("Reversed source targets retain strong adversarial derivatives")
      .save_svg("build/bigan_source_gradients.svg");
  auto       a = n::full({1, 1}, 80.).requires_grad_(), b = n::full({1, 1}, -80.).requires_grad_();
  auto       source      = n::models::LogisticGanObjectives(a, b);
  auto       loss        = n::mean(n::softplus(a)) + source.generator;
  const auto derivatives = n::grad(loss, {a, b});
  std::print(
      "Float32 at a80,b-80: EG loss={:.6f}; derivatives={},{}.\n",
      loss.item(),
      derivatives[0].item(),
      derivatives[1].item());
}
```
