> Verification and limits

The core-delegated unchanged original matches all nine archives / 6210 values, every dtype,
shape and value byte, both TSV files, the three original PNGs and non-timing JSON values.
The report matches the numerical artifacts and schedule; its PNG/SVG graphics are newly
annotated native plots, rather than byte-identical hand-drawn PNGs. Every original point,
fixed window and outside count is retained. Duration is remeasured.

The independent NumPy checker reads artifacts without datamunge or model fitting. It
checks 3398 forward values, 1312 exact procedural coordinates, 768 exact fixed/changed noise
values, all 1200 fitting rows and 32 scalar finite differences. Its first D-Adam replay uses
widened equations followed by Float32 parameter rounding before checking the 82 stored G
partials; that replay is approximate native rounding, not a bit-exact independent Adam
implementation. The native program separately checks E-independent G partials bit for bit.

Native/Python/R component checks cover ordered data, explicit joint pair parts, separate
writes, unequal pools, parameter census, owned fields, input validation and continuous
first/higher paths. This does not finish independent Python/R experiment loops. This single
fixed short run supplies no seed sweep, uncertainty estimate, paper image benchmark or
proof of distributional convergence. The failed inverse and concentration near the centre
remain visible parts of the result.

References: [Donahue, Krähenbühl and Darrell, Adversarial Feature Learning](https://arxiv.org/abs/1605.09782)
and [Dumoulin et al., Adversarially Learned Inference](https://arxiv.org/abs/1606.00704),
2016 preprints/2017 papers. Seeds, ordered ring, dimensions, schedule and artifacts follow
the original repository experiment; canonical native Gaussian, tanh/linear and logistic
components provide the reused computations.
